      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white border-b border-slate-800 pb-2">
        Resolving C-Extension &amp; Native Shared Library Linkage (`libpq`, OpenSSL 3, `libffi`)
      </h2>
      <p>
        The most notorious obstacle when bootstrapping Python developers is C-extension header missing errors during <code>pip install</code>. Common manifestations include:
      </p>
      <ul class="space-y-2 text-slate-300 list-disc pl-5">
        <li><code>fatal error: 'libpq-fe.h' file not found</code> (when compiling <code>psycopg2</code> for PostgreSQL).</li>
        <li><code>fatal error: 'openssl/ssl.h' file not found</code> (when building <code>cryptography</code> or <code>urllib3</code>).</li>
        <li><code>ld: library not found for -lffi</code> (when building foreign function interfaces).</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        In unmanaged operating systems, developers attempt to fix these by running <code>brew install postgresql</code> or <code>apt-get install libpq-dev</code>, which results in version drift across your team. In our Nix Flake template, the <code>shellHook</code> automatically constructs the <code>CPATH</code>, <code>LIBRARY_PATH</code>, and <code>LD_LIBRARY_PATH</code> environment variables referencing the exact nix store paths. The C-compiler locates all headers and dynamic libraries with zero host-level dependencies.
      </p>

